{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G2YUsCXpzZorS3T2CK8xjb","type":"tool_result","content":"93:The FP8-as-int8-SUM fast path (`:507-515`) is **introduced by this PR family** — see `pr54129.diff` hunk at `pr54129.diff:8770-8783`. [S] Rows are sharded across ranks (`num_embeddings_per_partition = divide(num_embeddings_padded, tp_size)`, `:313-314`), each vocab row has exactly one owner, so SUM reconstructs it.\n107:`MmapPleTable.__init__` memory-maps **every** shard with no rank filter: `ple_mmap.py:330-334` [S]:\n109:for idx, (path, offset, rows) in shards.items():\n113:and `_attach_table` *requires* all expected shards present per rank (`ple_mmap.py:870-878`, `missing = [...]` → `RuntimeError`). [S]\n133:**(b) shard tensors dropped on the floor** — `nvidia/ple_layer.py:517-527` [S]:\n137:    # this shard transiently, but it is never retained.\n142:`loaded_weight` is never copied anywhere; the reference dies at loop iteration end. **Confirmed: never materialized into a resident table.** Note the honest caveat in the PR's own comment: the *loader* still streams each shard into host memory transiently — the flag removes the resident copy, not the streaming transient. [S]\n144:The stock (env-off) path immediately below at `:533-541` is the only code that would touch `.weight`/`.shard_indices`; `MmapNgramEmbedding` deliberately has neither (`ple_mmap.py:507-511` docstring). [S]\n146:**Shard discovery.** `discover_shards(model_path)` — `ple_mmap.py:183-268`, `@functools.cache` at `:183`. [S]\n147:- Globs `os.path.join(model_path, \"*.safetensors\")` (`:209`) and parses each file's header (`:210`, `parse_safetensors_header` at `:125-170`).\n148:- Matches tensor **names** with `_SHARD_RE = re.compile(r\"layers\\.(\\d+)\\.ple\\.ple_embedding\\.ngram_embedding\\.shard_(\\d+)\\.weight$\")`, `ple_mmap.py:94-96`. [S]\n149:- Records `per_layer[layer_idx][shard_idx] = (path, data_start + start, rows)` (`:243-247`) — i.e. **(file path, absolute byte offset, row count)**.\n151:- No `model.safetensors.index.json` exists for this checkpoint, hence direct header parsing — `ple_mmap.py:122-123`. [CL — comment]\n153:**`shard_0` / `shard_1` are NOT TP shards. Confirmed.** [S] Precisely: `shard_i` is the *i*-th **row block of the logical table as stored under its own checkpoint tensor name**, produced by the checkpoint's `split_ngram_parts` split (default 512, `nvidia/ple_layer.py:241`). The mapping is `shard_size = (org_vocab_size + split_ngram_parts - 1) // split_ngram_parts; checkpoint_start = shard_index * shard_size` — written twice, verbatim, at `nvidia/ple_layer.py:502-509` (loader) and `ple_mmap.py:869-891` (`_attach_table`), with a comment at `ple_mmap.py:866-868` calling them \"two self-consistent halves of the same mapping\". [S] The runtime lookup is `shard = uniq // self.shard_size` (`ple_mmap.py:367`) — pure row arithmetic, no `tp_rank` anywhere. TP sharding in the stock path is an *orthogonal* second split applied by `VocabParallelEmbedding` (`vocab_parallel_embedding.py:313-314`), and `copy_ple_embedding_shard_` is what intersects the two (`nvidia/ple_layer.py:534-540`, `tp_start=/tp_end=` from `shard_indices`). [S]\n155:Nuance on the campaign's phrasing \"FILE shards\": a shard is a *named tensor*, and `discover_shards` does not require one file per shard — it records whichever file holds it. In the test fixture each shard happens to get its own file (`tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:112-124`, `model-ple-{layer_idx}-{shard_index:05d}.safetensors`). [S] So: \"checkpoint row-block tensors, located by file+offset\" is the exact statement; \"not TP shards\" is correct.\n157:Contract test: `test_shard_mapping_matches_upstream_checkpoint_math_at_boundaries`, `tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap.py:369-411`, drives real boundary rows through `MmapPleTable.gather` and compares against the logical table. [S]\n241:# Keep num_reqs-dependent ID generation outside PIECEWISE CUDA graphs,\n262:Call si